<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Akinyi wants to estimate the area of a plot near Nyeri. The plot lies between a straight road and a curved stream. Starting at one corner, she measured the width of the plot (from the road to the stream) every <m>{5}\,\text{m}</m> along the road, which is <m>{40}\,\text{m}</m> long.</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Distance along road (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{0}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{5}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{15}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{25}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{35}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Width (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{21}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{27}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{32}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{35}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{37}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{38}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{38}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{36}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{33}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>(a) Use the mid-ordinate rule with <m>{4}</m> strips, each <m>{10}\,\text{m}</m> wide, to estimate the area of the plot.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> <m>\text{m}^2</m>  </div><p>(b) Use the trapezoidal rule with the same <m>{4}</m> strips to estimate the area.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> <m>\text{m}^2</m>  </div><p>(c) The land is valued at Ksh <m>{2000}</m> per square metre. By how much do the two estimates of its value differ?</p><div>Ksh <fillin characters="10" name="ans3"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The <m>{4}</m> strips are <m>{10}\,\text{m}</m> wide, so their edges are at <m>0, {10}, {20}, \ldots, {40}\,\text{m}</m> and their middles at <m>{5}, {15}, {25}, \ldots\,\text{m}</m>.</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Distance (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{0}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{5}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{15}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{25}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{35}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Width (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{21}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{27}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{32}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{35}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{37}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{38}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{38}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{36}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{33}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Used as</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">edge</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">middle</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">edge</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">middle</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">edge</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">middle</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">edge</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">middle</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">edge</cell></row></tabular><p>(a) <m>M = {10} \times ({136}) = {1360}\,\text{m}^2</m>, using only the widths at the middles.</p><p>(b) <m>T = \tfrac{1}{2} \times {10} \times \left[{21} + {33} + 2 \times {107}\right] = {1340}\,\text{m}^2</m>, using only the widths at the edges.</p><p>(c) The values are Ksh <m>{2\,720\,000}</m> and Ksh <m>{2\,680\,000}</m>, so they differ by Ksh <m>{40\,000}</m> (that is, <m>{20}\,\text{m}^2 \times {2000}</m>).</p><p>The edge of the plot bends downwards, like an arch, so the trapezoidal estimate is too small and the mid-ordinate estimate too big: the true area lies between them.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>1360</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>1340</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>40000</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>